WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.940 --> 00:00:04.420
This is a folded example of a Miura-ori pattern.

00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:09.619
It is famous not just in origami but in engineering
as well.

00:00:09.619 --> 00:00:14.650
It is useful because it is contracting and
expanding.

00:00:14.650 --> 00:00:21.410
It is an example of a negative Poisson ratio
material.

00:00:21.410 --> 00:00:28.140
So there has been some interest in using this
as a kind of meta material for which you could

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:33.650
tune its properties, in particular its compressibility
properties.

00:00:33.650 --> 00:00:39.780
The pure Miura-ori compresses like so, but
you can introduce defects.

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:43.140
I've introduced a defect here in this corner.

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:51.550
And in the area of the defect, the paper is
distorted a bit and it changes the compressibility

00:00:51.550 --> 00:00:56.660
properties of this pattern as a material.

00:00:56.660 --> 00:01:01.579
So what happens if you introduce a number
of these defects?

00:01:01.579 --> 00:01:06.860
What happens when you flood this thing with
defects?

00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:09.600
Will it still fold up in the same way?

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:15.080
My research is centered on quantifying the
effect of defects, how neighboring defects

00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:21.300
interact with each other, and at what point
do you loose all the long-range order in this

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:24.110
material.

00:01:24.110 --> 00:01:31.240
In order to do that, I related origami to
an area of physics named statistical mechanics.

00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:39.050
I considered each of the defects as an atom
that can interact with its neighbors.

00:01:39.050 --> 00:01:45.660
With a sufficient number of atoms interacting,
you can show that the Miura-ori has a phase

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:51.610
transition, a point at which all of the long-range
order in the material vanishes.

